+# ADR-0013: Capability-scoped view-contract scenarios
+
+## Status
+Accepted (2026-11-10)
+
+Refines [ADR-0005](0005-view-registry.md): the view-contract scenarios still run for every registered view, except the scenarios of a capability the view does not show.
+
+## Context
+
+[ADR-0005](0005-view-registry.md) says the view-contract BDD E2E scenarios "run for every registered view" and that a new view cannot skip them. Its only exclusion is a pending tag, meant for a scenario a view cannot pass yet.
+
+[ADR-0011](0011-meeting-range-in-grid-view.md) gives the meeting range to Grid only: "Cards ignore the range". The meeting scenarios (set, resize, share and clear) can never apply to Cards, so a pending tag, which promises that the scenario comes back, does not fit.
+
+## Decision
+
+A contract scenario that carries a capability tag (today `@meeting-range`) runs for every view whose registry entry declares that capability (`showsMeetingRange`) and for no other view. It is not a pending exclusion. A view that declares the capability cannot skip the scenario.
+
+`playwright.bdd.config.ts` derives each view's `grepInvert` from the registry flag, with no list of view ids.
+
+## Consequences
+Positive:
+- A future view that sets `showsMeetingRange` runs the meeting scenarios with no config edit.
+- The contract run of Cards stays green without a pending tag.
+
+Negative:
+- The registry flag now also scopes tests, so removing it from Grid silently drops the scenario. The `--list` counts of the change guard this.
+
+## Alternatives Considered
+**A pending tag for Cards**: it says "not yet", while Cards never gets the range.
+
+**Record the rule in ADR-0011**: ADR-0011 decides the meeting range, not how contract scenarios run.
+
+## References
+`openspec/changes/add-grid-meeting-range-and-share` (D11).
